---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import dna from '../data/dna.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const expected = [
  ['Ashkenazi Jewish', '~25%', 'from Jacob Leibholz, of Schubin and Berlin-Schöneberg', '0.0%'],
  ['Germanic / Baltic', '~25%', 'from Auguste Luwinski, of Sauerbaum in East Prussia', '1.4%'],
  ['Portuguese', '~50%', 'from Ivone de Figueiredo, of Lourenço Marques', '60.6%'],
];
const matches = [
  ['A match in the United Kingdom', '2nd cousin', 333.9, 'British cluster'],
  ['A match in the United Kingdom', "1st cousin's daughter", 297.5, 'British cluster — South Africa in common'],
  ['A match in Australia', '2nd cousin', 292.7, 'British cluster — manages a tree of 10,715'],
  ['A match in the United Kingdom', "2nd cousin's son", 165.9, 'British cluster'],
  ['A match in Portugal', '3rd cousin', 133.2, 'Portuguese cluster — Portugal in common'],
  ['A match in Portugal', "2nd cousin's daughter", 111.2, 'Portuguese cluster — Portugal in common'],
  ['A match in Portugal', "2nd cousin's son", 108.7, 'Portuguese cluster — Portugal in common'],
  ['A match, manager in Japan', "2nd cousin's son", 99.1, ''],
  ['A match in the United Kingdom', "2nd cousin's son", 96.0, 'British cluster'],
  ['A match in the United Kingdom', "2nd cousin's son", 92.5, 'British cluster'],
];
---
<Base title="What the DNA says"
  subtitle="A test the paper record set for itself, and failed"
  lede="The family tree makes a hard, checkable prediction about Derrick's DNA. The DNA does not meet it — not narrowly, but by the whole of one grandparent.">

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The finding <span class="tag inf">Inferred, strongly</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Kurt Israel Luwinski is very probably not Lynn Derrick Luwinski's biological father.</strong>
      Two independent lines of evidence say so, and the paper record was already hinting at it before any
      test was taken.
    </p>
    <p class="small">Not <em>documented</em>, and not closed. What would close it is set out at the foot of this page.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The prediction, and the result</h2>
  <p>
    If Derrick is Kurt's son, then Kurt's father Jacob Leibholz — a Jewish merchant of Schubin and
    Schöneberg — must put roughly a quarter Ashkenazi Jewish into Derrick's genome, and Kurt's mother
    Auguste, of an East Prussian farming family, roughly a quarter Germanic. Neither is optional. This
    is simply how autosomal inheritance works over two generations.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>From</th><th>Expected</th><th>Why</th><th>Observed</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{expected.map(([g, e, w, o]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{g}</strong></td><td>{e}</td><td class="small">{w}</td><td><strong>{o}</strong></td></tr>
    ))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    Ashkenazi Jewish is the most sharply resolved population in consumer ancestry testing. A strong
    founder effect and centuries of endogamy make the signal unmistakable, and a quarter-share is not
    something an estimate mislays. <strong>Zero is not noise.</strong> Neither is 1.4% standing in for
    an East Prussian grandmother.
  </p>

  <h2>The full estimate</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Population</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {dna.europe.map((e) => (<tr><td>{e.group}</td><td><strong>{e.pct}%</strong></td></tr>))}
      <tr><td class="muted">Ashkenazi Jewish</td><td class="muted"><strong>0.0%</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Additional genetic groups reported: <strong>{dna.additional_genetic_groups.join('</strong>, <strong>')}</strong>.
    Everything but the Portuguese — 39.4% of the whole — is northwest European and leans English.
  </p>

  <h2>The match list says it independently</h2>
  <p>
    Ethnicity estimates are inferences. A match list is arithmetic on shared segments, and it is harder
    to argue with. Derrick has <strong>5,464 matches</strong>. The ten closest fall into two clean
    groups and neither of them is German or Jewish.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Match</th><th>Estimated relationship</th><th>Shared cM</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{matches.map(([m, r, cm, n]) => (
      <tr><td>{m}</td><td>{r}</td><td><strong>{cm}</strong></td><td class="small muted">{n}</td></tr>
    ))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Match holders are living people and are not named here. Their names are in the private research file.
  </p>
  <p>
    A person with one Ashkenazi grandparent normally carries <em>thousands</em> of Ashkenazi matches,
    because that population is heavily over-represented in these databases. Derrick has none at the top
    of his list. What he has instead is a British cluster — one of whom shares South Africa with him as
    an ancestral place — and the Portuguese cluster that is his mother, exactly where she should be.
  </p>

  <h2>The paper record was already saying it</h2>
  <blockquote>
    Ivone married Johannes Antonie Oelofse de Witt on 2 February 1948.<br />
    Ivone married Kurt Luwinski on 19 June 1948, entered in the register as <em>Ivone de Witt</em>.<br />
    Kurt filed for divorce <em>after June 1948</em> — within weeks of the wedding, and before the child was born.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    None of that was read as meaning anything until the DNA arrived. Read backwards from the DNA it is
    hard to read as anything else. <a href={base + '/1948/'}>The year, set out day by day →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What this does not touch</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ivone stands.</strong> Three Portuguese matches at 108–133 cM confirm her line directly.</li>
    <li><strong>The Berlin history stands.</strong> Jacob Leibholz was deported to Auschwitz on 26 February 1943. Samuel died there, Oskar died there, Georg died at Chełmno. Kurt escaped in 1933. All of it is documented and none of it depends on this question.</li>
    <li><strong>It remains Derrick's family.</strong> He was born into it, named for it, and raised in it. This archive keeps descent and inheritance in separate columns rather than collapsing one into the other.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Nor does de Witt obviously fit</h3>
    <p>
      The temptation is to move the fatherhood four months earlier and be done. But an Afrikaner
      Oelofse–van Straaten line should read Dutch, German and French Huguenot. The observed Dutch is
      <strong>1.5%</strong> and Germanic <strong>1.4%</strong>, against English <strong>18.5%</strong>
      and a named genetic group of <em>Southeastern and Eastern England</em>. That is an English family,
      not a Cape one. The question is open, and this archive does not intend to close it by guessing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The British cluster, worked</h2>
  <p>
    Three of the closest matches have now been opened in full. The structure that came out changes
    this page from a negative finding into a positive one. Match holders are living and are not named
    here; what follows is the shape, and the family surnames, which are the search terms.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Match</th><th>Shared</th><th>Reading</th><th>Their surnames</th><th>Shared matches</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A man in the United Kingdom</td><td><strong>333.9 cM</strong></td><td><strong>2nd cousin, 55.9%</strong> — MRCA <strong>great-grandparents</strong></td><td><strong>Harrison · Brown</strong></td><td><strong>124</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A woman in the United Kingdom</td><td><strong>297.5 cM</strong></td><td><strong>1st cousin's daughter, 39.7%</strong> — MRCA <strong>grandparents</strong></td><td><strong>Halsey · Parr · Fenton</strong></td><td><strong>103</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A woman in Australia</td><td><strong>292.7 cM</strong></td><td><strong>2nd cousin, 68.0%</strong> — MRCA great-grandparents</td><td>a 10,715-person English tree</td><td><strong>149</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>It is one family, and it is tightly interlocked</h3>
  <p>
    Each shared-match table gives a person's relationship to <em>both</em> sides at once, which is what
    makes the shape readable. Five of these people — Harrison, Browne, Noye, Taw, Davey — are
    <strong>first cousins to one another</strong>, sharing between <strong>450 and 801 cM</strong>. And
    every one of them is a <strong>second cousin to Derrick</strong>. That is not a scatter of
    coincidental matches. It is one English family, and Derrick hangs off it at a fixed distance.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And one match is a generation closer <span class="tag inf">The lead</span></h3>
    <p>
      The 297.5 cM match is <em>not</em> part of that knot — to her, the Harrison line is only a
      <em>parent's third cousin</em>. She sits nearer to Derrick than any of them:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>1st cousin's daughter — 39.7% — most recent common ancestors: Derrick's grandparents.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      In plain terms: <strong>her grandparent was a sibling of Derrick's biological father.</strong>
      And the detail that turns arithmetic into a place —
      <strong>her father was born in Johannesburg</strong>. Her family surnames are
      <strong>Halsey</strong>, <strong>Parr</strong> and <strong>Fenton</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>What that establishes, and what it doesn't</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The paternal family is English</strong> — which the ethnicity estimate said, and the match structure now confirms independently and at close range.</li>
    <li><strong>A branch of it was in Johannesburg</strong>, in the right city at the right time.</li>
    <li><strong>The nearest surname is Halsey</strong>, at grandparent distance. Harrison, Brown, Davey, Noye and Taw sit one generation further out.</li>
    <li><strong>It closes the Kurt question from the other side.</strong> Every one of these pages prints the same line — <em>“do not share any ancestral surnames”</em> — against Derrick's own list of Figueiredo, Leibholz, Luwinski, Schmul, Thimm. Six matches examined closely, three British and three Portuguese, and not one touches Leibholz or Luwinski.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>It does not name him.</strong> A first-cousin-once-removed relationship fixes a distance,
    not a person, and two shapes still fit equally: Derrick's father was a <strong>Halsey</strong> of
    Johannesburg, brother to that match's grandfather — or he married a Halsey sister, and the name
    to find is <strong>Parr</strong> or <strong>Fenton</strong>. Chromosome triangulation between the
    two clusters would separate them.
  </p>

  <h2>Triangulation: it is one family, not two</h2>
  <p>
    The paragraph above left two shapes open — a Halsey father, or a Halsey sister married to
    someone else. Segment coordinates settle it. The three matches share, respectively, 12, 16 and 12
    segments with Derrick; laid over each other they produce this:
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Chromosome 2, 181,681,048 – 216,191,586 <span class="tag doc">34.5 Mb</span></h3>
    <p><strong>All three matches share that exact stretch with Derrick.</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Overlapping regions</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The two second cousins, with each other</td><td>9</td><td><strong>115.0 Mb</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>The closest match × the Australian one</td><td>1</td><td>45.3 Mb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The closest match × the English one</td><td>2</td><td>38.2 Mb</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    The two second cousins overlapping across 115 Mb is just what first cousins look like — they
    already share 801 cM with each other. <strong>The finding is the closest match joining them on
    the same stretch of chromosome 2.</strong> The alternative shape would have put her on a
    different chromosome from them. She is on the same one.
  </p>
  <p>
    So <strong>Derrick's biological father belongs to one English family</strong>, and
    <strong>Halsey is the nearest surname on it</strong> — that match's grandparent was his father's
    sibling. Harrison and Brown sit a generation further out on the same line.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And a recombination you can see</h3>
    <p>
      On chromosome 18 one match's segment ends at <strong>55,491,965</strong> and the other's begins
      at <strong>55,493,868</strong>. <strong>They abut, 1,903 base pairs apart.</strong> Two
      relatives holding adjacent, non-overlapping stretches of the same ancestral chromosome is the
      signature of a break in a shared ancestor — the point where one grandparent's chromosome was
      cut and handed down two different branches. Between them, the two matches reconstruct nearly
      the whole of Derrick's paternal chromosome 18.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    <strong>The honest caveat:</strong> strict triangulation also requires the three matches to share
    that segment <em>with each other</em>, which needs their kits compared directly and only they can
    run that. The pairwise totals make it very likely. The conclusion does not rest on it, because the
    relationship estimates already place all three on one side and the overlap confirms the side is
    shared. <span class="tag inf">Inferred, strongly</span>
  </p>

  <h2>The cluster mapped from the inside <span class="tag doc">New, 14 September 2026</span></h2>
  <p>
    Until now this archive knew the British matches only by their distance from <strong>Derrick</strong>.
    But each match's own review page also gives every shared match's distance from <strong>that
    match</strong>. Measure the cluster from two vantage points at once and its shape falls out.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Shared match</th><th>with Derrick</th><th>with the 10,715-person tree</th><th>with the closest match</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The Harrison match</td><td>333.9 cM</td><td><strong>801.0 cM — 1st cousin</strong></td><td>73.4 cM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A second UK match</td><td>165.9 cM</td><td><strong>714.0 cM — 1st cousin</strong></td><td>60.7 cM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A third</td><td>99.1 cM</td><td>571.3 cM</td><td>69.5 cM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A fourth</td><td>88.6 cM</td><td>480.3 cM</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>The closest match</strong></td><td><strong>297.5 cM</strong></td><td><strong>82.1 cM</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>One line, read at two depths <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      Four of those people are <strong>first cousins and their children</strong> to the holder of the big
      tree — 480 to 801 cM with her. They are one tight family, and Derrick stands at second-cousin
      distance from all of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The closest match inverts the ratio.</strong> She is 297.5 cM to Derrick and only 60–82 cM
      to every member of that core. She is not part of it — she is <em>nearer to Derrick than they are.</em>
      That is not two families. It is one line, read at two depths:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The closest match's common ancestors with Derrick are his own grandparents.</strong> She was born in 1995, two generations below him, and MyHeritage reads her as a first cousin's granddaughter.</li>
      <li><strong>The big-tree holder's are one generation further back</strong> — his great-grandparents. Second cousin, 68%.</li>
      <li><strong>Which predicts that those two should be third cousins to each other.</strong> They are, at 82.1 cM. The structure is consistent from three directions at once, and that is the strongest thing about it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Two surnames, at their two depths <span class="tag inf">The short list</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>At grandparent depth: Halsey.</strong> The closest match's own pedigree is
      <em>Halsey · Halsey · Parr · Parr · Fenton</em> — every one of them living, and named nowhere here.
      Her match card carries the line that matters: <em>“Ancestral places common to derrick luwinski and
      [her] include <strong>South Africa</strong>.”</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>At great-grandparent depth: Brown, Castle and Webb</strong> — documented four generations up
      in a tree of 10,715 people, and all of these people are dead, so the archive can name them:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Named in the tree</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Parents</td><td><strong>Geoffrey David Brown</strong>, 1926–1980 · <strong>Hazel Webb</strong>, 1931–2022</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Grandparents</td><td><strong>Walter George Brown</strong>, 1889–1971 · <strong>Agnes Kate Castle</strong>, 1891–1967 · <strong>Dorothy May Webb</strong>, 1907–1990 · and a grandfather recorded only as <strong>Unknown</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Great-grandparents</td><td><strong>Alfred John Brown</strong> 1853–1924 &amp; <strong>Sarah Maria Brister</strong> b.1858 · <strong>George William Castle</strong> 1862–1917 &amp; <strong>Caroline Wallis</strong> 1862–1957 · <strong>William Frederick Webb</strong> 1877–1955 &amp; <strong>Louisa Hayden</strong> 1880–1965</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Beyond</td><td>Henry George Brown 1827–1891 · Ann E. Woodhouse 1828–1884 · Henry Brister 1823–1915 · Mary Warnes 1818–1884 · George Castle 1840–1885 · Martha Giddings 1841–1863 · Joseph Wallis 1827–1912 · Sarah Ann Field 1830–1891 · William Thomas Webb 1842–1921 · Fanny Hawkins 1847–1932 · David Hayden 1848–1927 · Alice Lucas 1854–1922</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>Second cousins have parents who were first cousins.</strong> So if that reading holds,
      <strong>Derrick's biological father was a first cousin of Geoffrey David Brown or of Hazel Webb</strong>
      — a man of that generation, carrying on the other evidence the surname <strong>Halsey</strong>, and in
      <strong>Johannesburg</strong> in 1948. And Derrick's own great-grandparents are one of four couples:
      Brown–Brister, Castle–Wallis, Webb–Hayden, or the unrecorded couple behind that
      <strong>Unknown</strong> grandfather.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <s>That last one is worth staring at. In a documented tree of 10,715 people, one grandfather is
      recorded as “Unknown” — in exactly the generation, and exactly the position, where an undocumented
      connection would sit.</s> <strong>Set aside the following day — see below.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>This is a structure, not a name</strong>, and it is labelled Inferred. What it does is turn an
      open question into a short list: <strong>four couples, one surname, one city, one decade.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Halved again, and the “Unknown” grandfather drops out <span class="tag inf">15 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      The short list ran to four great-grandparent couples, and the archive made a point of the fourth —
      a grandfather recorded only as <em>Unknown</em> in a tree of ten thousand people. <strong>That was
      the wrong one to stare at,</strong> and one more look at the match pages says so.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two people who are <strong>first cousins</strong> to the big-tree holder — 801 cM and 714 cM
      with her, and Derrick's first and third closest British matches at 333.9 and 165.9 cM — both list
      <strong>Brown</strong> among their ancestral surnames. One has <em>Brown, Harrison</em>; the other
      has <em>Arrow, Brown, Noye</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      First cousins share one grandparent couple. If both of hers carry Brown, they are on her
      <strong>father's</strong> side — descended from <strong>Walter George Brown and Agnes Kate
      Castle</strong>. And if Derrick is a second cousin to all three of them, then his own
      great-grandparents are the parents of one of <em>that</em> couple:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Still possible</th><th>Now unlikely</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Alfred John Brown</strong> 1853–1924 &amp; <strong>Sarah Maria Brister</strong> b.1858</td>
          <td>William Frederick Webb 1877–1955 &amp; Louisa Hayden 1880–1965</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>George William Castle</strong> 1862–1917 &amp; <strong>Caroline Wallis</strong> 1862–1957</td>
          <td>The unrecorded couple behind the <strong>“Unknown”</strong> grandfather</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>So Derrick's biological grandparent was a sibling of Walter George Brown or of Agnes Kate
      Castle</strong>, and his biological father was that sibling's son — a man born around 1910 to 1928
      who was in Johannesburg in 1948, carrying on the other evidence the surname <strong>Halsey</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Held as Inferred, and here are its weaknesses.</strong> <em>Brown</em> is among the commonest
      surnames in England, and both trees are tiny — three people and five. A relationship estimate of
      “second cousin” carries real width; the same 333.9 cM also reads as half first cousin at 21%. What
      makes it worth acting on is that <strong>two independent first cousins point the same way</strong>,
      and that it is falsifiable: one reply naming a Webb connection would overturn it in a sentence.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Eight names <span class="tag doc">15 September 2026</span></h2>
  <p>
    A fair question was put to this archive: if one of the British matches keeps a tree on Ancestry,
    are the same people not also in MyHeritage's records — and had anyone looked? <strong>They had
    not.</strong> Only the DNA match list had been searched by surname, never the record collections
    and never the matches' own trees. Looking took twenty minutes and produced the best result of the
    project.
  </p>
  <p>
    The record collections were the wrong door — <em>Brown</em> returns 1.3 million hits and the search
    form resists filtering. <strong>The right door was the match's own tree, which has been open the
    whole time:</strong> 10,715 people, on MyHeritage, viewable, and it lists siblings as well as
    ancestors.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>First, a confirmation nobody had to argue for</h3>
    <p>
      Among the children of <strong>Walter George Brown</strong> (b. Great Yarmouth 1889, d. Hampshire
      1971) is a daughter, <strong>Decima Audrey Harrison, 1928–1997</strong>. A Brown daughter who
      married a Harrison. <strong>That is where the Harrison match comes from</strong>, and it settles
      by documentation what was inferred yesterday from centimorgans alone: he is on the Brown side.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And then the short list itself <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      If Derrick is a second cousin to the tree's keeper, his biological grandparent was a
      <strong>sibling of Walter George Brown or of Agnes Kate Castle</strong>. Both sets are now named.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Children of <strong>Alfred John Brown</strong> (1853–1924) &amp; <strong>Sarah Maria Brister</strong> (b.1858)</th><th>Children of <strong>George William Castle</strong> (1862–1917) &amp; <strong>Caroline Wallis</strong> (1862–1957)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Archibald Alfred Brown</strong> 1879–1959</td><td><strong>George W. Castle</strong> b.1886</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Frank Henry Brown</strong> b.1880</td><td><strong>Ada Castle</strong> b.1887</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Alice Brown</strong> b.1884</td><td><strong>Frederick Castle</strong> b.1890</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ernest H. Brown</strong> 1897–1972</td><td><strong>Martha M. Castle</strong> b.1896</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="small muted">(and Walter George Brown 1889–1971)</td><td class="small muted">(and Agnes Kate Castle 1891–1967)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>Eight people.</strong> Derrick's biological father was a child of one of them, born
      roughly 1910 to 1928, and in <strong>Johannesburg</strong> in 1948. The family is
      <strong>Norfolk</strong> — Norwich, Great Yarmouth, Caister-on-Sea, and Alfred John Brown was born
      at a hamlet called <em>California</em> on the Norfolk coast.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Superseded on 15 September.</strong> Two things above are wrong. The <strong>Castles are
      Hertfordshire</strong>, not Norfolk — only the Browns are Norfolk — and the census puts Derrick's
      father a generation higher than “a child of one of them”. The four Brown siblings are no longer
      part of his descent at all. <a href="#castle">What replaced this →</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Which makes one thing suddenly obvious <span class="tag inf">The hypothesis to test</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Three of those eight are daughters</strong> — Alice Brown, Ada Castle, Martha M. Castle.
      A daughter's sons do not carry her maiden name. <strong>They carry her husband's.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      And the surname sitting on Derrick's single closest match, whose shared ancestral place with him
      is South Africa, is <strong>Halsey</strong>.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>If Alice Brown, Ada Castle or Martha Castle married a Halsey</strong>, then her sons were
      Halseys, born in exactly the right years — and one of them was Derrick's biological father.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      It also puts <strong>Mervin Halsey</strong>, the grandfather the closest match named, exactly
      where he needs to be: <strong>a brother of Derrick's father</strong>, another son of that same
      marriage.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>This is checkable without anybody's permission</strong> — so it was checked, the same
      afternoon, in the England and Wales marriage index. <strong>The entry is not there.</strong>
      See below.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Tested the same afternoon, and it fails <span class="tag disp">Negative</span></h3>
    <p>
      The Halsey hypothesis was published and then put to the England and Wales marriage index within
      the hour. It does not survive.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Searched, 1895–1935</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Halsey × Brown</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong> — Pancras 1907, Barnet 1908, Watford 1917, Newport Pagnell 1923. <strong>None in Norfolk</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Alice Brown × Halsey</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Halsey × Castle</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Parr × Castle</td><td>2, both London, neither fits</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>No daughter of these two families married a Halsey in England or Wales.</strong> The neat
      join between the tree on one side and the surname on the other is not in the index, and the
      archive says so rather than leaving a tidy guess standing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What survives, and what it means.</strong> The eight names came from the tree, not from
      the hypothesis, and they stand. So does something the tree shows plainly:
      <strong>Alice Brown, Ada Castle and Martha M. Castle have no spouse and no children recorded at
      all</strong> — only parents and siblings. They are genuinely untraced, which is exactly where an
      unrecorded branch would sit. That is not evidence <em>for</em> the hypothesis; it is the absence
      of evidence against it.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Three ways it could still be true, and one way it could be wrong from the start.</strong>
      The marriage may have happened in <strong>South Africa</strong>, outside the English index
      entirely. The Halsey name may enter a generation lower. Or — and this is the assumption worth
      naming — <strong>Halsey is simply the closest match's father's surname.</strong> Her link to
      Derrick runs through one of her four grandparents, and it could as easily be the
      <strong>Parr</strong> side, or her grandmother <strong>Cynthia Weir</strong>. This archive picked
      Halsey because it was the name on the card. <span class="tag open">Open</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Johannesburg Halseys, documented at last <span class="tag doc">15 September 2026</span></h2>
  <p>
    This archive has said for weeks that the closest match's surname was <strong>Halsey</strong> and
    that <em>“her family branch was in Johannesburg.”</em> That was an inference drawn from a line on a
    DNA match card. <strong>It is now a marriage certificate.</strong>
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The grandparents she named a year ago, found</h3>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Groom</strong></td><td><strong>Mervin Richard Halsey</strong>, aged <strong>22</strong>, born about <strong>1930</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Bride</strong></td><td><strong>Cynthia Yvonne Elizabeth Wear</strong>, aged 21</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Married</strong></td><td><strong>29 November 1952</strong>, at <strong>St Martin's-in-the-Veld, Johannesburg, Transvaal</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certificate</td><td><strong>51</strong> · <em>South Africa, Civil Marriage Records</em>, FamilySearch <code>1:1:6ZH5-65NP</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Image</td><td>Digital folder <strong>008109684</strong>, image <strong>2149 of 2,685</strong> — <em>“Johannesburg. Marriage Certificates 1952”</em></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      In June 2025 the closest match wrote that her paternal grandparents were <em>“Mervin and Cynthia
      Halsey — her maiden name was Weir … Cynthia's middle name was Yvonne.”</em> <strong>Every
      particular of that checks out</strong>, down to the middle name; the register spells the maiden
      name <strong>Wear</strong> rather than Weir.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the Johannesburg cemetery records carry them both to the end:
      <strong>Mervin Richard Halsey</strong>, born 15 June 1930, died <strong>30 May 2005</strong>,
      buried in Johannesburg — indexed there under the given name <em>Kevin</em>, which is the same man
      and the same middle name and year, mis-read. <strong>Cynthia Yvonne Elizabeth Halsey</strong>,
      born 1932, died <strong>4 April 2000</strong>, buried at <strong>Brixton, Johannesburg</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      A supporting negative: there is <strong>no Mervin Halsey in the England and Wales birth index for
      1900–1940</strong>. He was not an Englishman who emigrated. <strong>He was South African.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Why his parents are now the whole question <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Mervin was born in 1930</strong>, a generation ahead of Derrick. MyHeritage reads the closest match as
      Derrick's <em>first cousin's granddaughter</em>, and she is Mervin's granddaughter — which puts
      <strong>Mervin himself as Derrick's first cousin.</strong>
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      If that holds, <strong>Mervin Richard Halsey's parent was a brother or sister of Derrick's
      biological father.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      One generation, one document. Three ways to get it, in order of likelihood:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The 1952 register itself</strong>, image 2149. The 1956 form has columns for the parents of both parties; the 1948 form has none. <strong>1952 falls between them</strong>, and the frame is already located.</li>
      <li><strong>His estate.</strong> He died in May 2005, so there should be a J294 naming his father and mother. It is not in the gazette corpus under that spelling — but the Master of the High Court can be asked.</li>
      <li><strong>His birth</strong>, South Africa, about 1930.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">
      And the question already put to her this morning — <em>who were Mervin Halsey's parents?</em> —
      turns out to have been exactly the right one, asked before any of this was known.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="castle">Castle, in Hertfordshire, twice over <span class="tag doc">15 September 2026</span></h2>
  <p>
    This page spent a week on the surname <strong>Halsey</strong>. It was the name on the closest
    match's card, and the archive followed it into the England and Wales marriage index, found nothing,
    published the negative, and kept going anyway. <strong>The negative was right and the reason was
    better than expected: there was never a Halsey marriage to find.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    The 1952 Johannesburg register was opened at full resolution this morning, hoping for columns naming
    the parents. <strong>The 1952 form has none.</strong> What it has instead is column (4),
    <em>Country of Birth — Province. Place.</em> — and that column ended the Halsey line and opened the
    real one in the same glance.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Two words in a column nobody was looking at</h3>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Column (4), as written</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Mervin Richard Halsey</strong>, 22½</td><td><strong>Cape Province</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cynthia Yvonne Elizabeth Wear</strong>, 21½</td><td><strong>ENGLAND</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      Derrick's cluster is English. Mervin was born in the <strong>Cape</strong>, and there is no
      Hertfordshire, Norfolk or anywhere-else-English in him. <strong>His wife was the one born in
      England</strong> — and the match descends from both of them equally. The archive had simply
      picked the man's surname because it was the one printed on the card.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The chain, in five documents</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Document</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>1</strong></td>
        <td><strong>GRO birth index, Q2 1931</strong><br/><span class="small muted">Hitchin, Hertfordshire · vol 3A p.1345</span></td>
        <td><strong>WEAR, Cynthia Y E.</strong> Mother's maiden name: <strong>CASTLE</strong>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>2</strong></td>
        <td><strong>GRO marriage index, Q4 1914</strong><br/><span class="small muted">Hitchin · vol 3A p.1552</span></td>
