  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Three things that were wrong on this page until today <span class="tag disp">Corrected</span></h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Castles were never in Norfolk.</strong> This page said “Norwich and Caister-on-Sea”. That is the <strong>Brown</strong> side. The Castles are <strong>Baldock and Norton, Hertfordshire</strong>, and Caroline came from Weston, four miles away.</li>
      <li><strong>Caroline Wallis is Caroline <em>Walls</em></strong> — her widowed father Joseph Walls, born at Weston in 1827, is living in the house in 1911.</li>
      <li><strong>The list of eight was half wrong.</strong> Brown enters this family only as the man Agnes Kate Castle married. The four Brown siblings drop out of Derrick's descent entirely.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the arithmetic changes which generation he sits in <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      Two of Derrick's closest matches now descend from <strong>one couple</strong>, through
      <strong>two different daughters</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>297.5 cM</strong></td><td>through <strong>Martha Maud Castle</strong> → Cynthia Wear → Halsey, Johannesburg</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>292.7 cM</strong></td><td>through <strong>Agnes Kate Castle</strong> → Walter George Brown</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>Five centimorgans apart, down two separate branches.</strong> That symmetry is the whole
      argument: Derrick is not hanging off one twig of this family, he is equidistant from two — which
      puts him <em>inside</em> the Baldock household rather than beside it.
    </p>
    <p>
      And it moves him up a generation. If Derrick's biological <em>grandparent</em> were a Castle
      sibling, the closest match would be a first cousin three times removed — about
      <strong>117 cM</strong> expected. The reading is <strong>297.5</strong>. If his biological
      <em>father</em> was himself a son of that house, the match is a first cousin twice removed,
      expected about 212 cM — and that is also, word for word, the relationship MyHeritage printed on
      the card: <em>first cousin's granddaughter</em>.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Derrick's biological grandparents are, on this reading, named for the first time:
      George Castle, born Baldock 1863, and Caroline Walls, born Weston 1863, married 1883.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Walked back within the hour.</strong> The paragraph above rests on the 292.7 cM match
      being a <em>grandchild</em> of Agnes Kate Castle. Her tree was then opened and she is a
      <em>great</em>-grandchild — her father Geoffrey David Brown, born 1926, is Agnes Kate's son. That
      is one generation further out than the arithmetic above assumed, and it changes the answer from
      settled to open. <a href="#rung">The corrected version is immediately below.</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rung">Which rung — an overreach, corrected the same hour <span class="tag disp">Corrected</span></h2>
  <p>
    The section above was published saying Derrick's biological father was <strong>a son</strong> of
    George Castle and Caroline Walls. That was stated with more confidence than the evidence carries,
    and the error was mine: I assumed the 292.7 cM match stood one generation closer to the Baldock
    couple than she does. <strong>Her tree, opened afterwards, puts her a generation further out.</strong>
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Descends from George &amp; Caroline as</th><th>Shares with Derrick</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The 292.7 cM match</td><td><strong>Great</strong>-grandchild — her father Geoffrey David Brown <span class="yr">1926–1980</span> is Agnes Kate Castle's son</td><td><strong>292.7 cM</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>The 333.9 cM match</td><td><strong>Great</strong>-grandchild — through Agnes Kate's daughter Decima Audrey Harrison <span class="yr">1928–1997</span></td><td><strong>333.9 cM</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>The 297.5 cM match</td><td><strong>Great-great</strong>-grandchild — through Martha Maud, then Cynthia Wear</td><td><strong>297.5 cM</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Two readings, and the numbers do not cleanly separate them</h3>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>A.</strong> His father was a <em>son</em> of the house</th><th><strong>B.</strong> His father was a <em>grandson</em></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>292.7 observed</td><td>expected <strong>425</strong> · ratio 0.69</td><td>expected <strong>229</strong> · ratio 1.28</td></tr>
        <tr><td>333.9 observed</td><td>expected <strong>425</strong> · ratio 0.79</td><td>expected <strong>229</strong> · ratio 1.46</td></tr>
        <tr><td>297.5 observed</td><td>expected <strong>212</strong> · ratio 1.40</td><td>expected <strong>122</strong> · ratio 2.44</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Total error</strong></td><td><strong>0.95</strong> — the better fit</td><td>1.51</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Father's age in June 1948</td><td><strong>44 to 63</strong> — uncomfortable</td><td><strong>20 to 38</strong> — natural</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MyHeritage's own label</td><td>calls the 297.5 match a <em>first cousin's granddaughter</em></td><td>calls the other two <em>second cousins</em></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>A fits the centimorgans better. B fits the ages better. MyHeritage's own algorithm returns
      one of each.</strong> And something in the readings is odd on either account: the two
      great-grandchildren average 313 cM and the great-<em>great</em>-grandchild reads 297.5, when a
      whole generation should roughly halve it. Two matches a generation apart at almost the same
      number is not what a simple line of descent produces.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the honest statement is narrower than the one published an hour earlier, and it is this:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Derrick descends from George Castle and Caroline Walls of Baldock.</strong> Whether they
      are his grandparents or his great-grandparents is not settled, and centimorgans alone will not
      settle it.
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">
      Under <strong>A</strong> his father is <strong>George W. Castle</strong> b.1885 or
      <strong>Frederick Castle</strong> b.1890 — 63 and 58 in 1948.
      <strong>Ernest</strong> b. 28 November 1904 is out: the 1939 Register has him at 29 Norton
      Crescent, Baldock, a <em>butcher (incapacitated)</em>, married, with children, which matches the
      “invalid” of 1911 and does not match a man in Johannesburg nine years later.
      Under <strong>B</strong> his father is one of the grandchildren of that house, born roughly 1910
      to 1928 — which is the shape the archive had a week ago, before the surname changed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What became of the Halseys, for the record <span class="tag doc">NAAIRS, 54 documents</span></h3>
    <p>
      They are no longer Derrick's family, but they were chased for a week and the findings should not
      simply vanish. The South African national archives index returns, under <strong>Halsey</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>WLD 12617/1978 — divorce: Mervin Richard Halsey versus Cynthia Yvonne Elizabeth.</strong> They separated in 1978, twenty-six years after St Martin's-in-the-Veld.</li>
      <li><strong>MHG 6348/64 — Erral David Halsey</strong>, the estate of the 25-year-old who died at Walvis Bay in July 1964, born at Spaarwater, Heidelberg, Transvaal.</li>
      <li><strong>MHG 790/55 — Penrose Thomas Halsey</strong>, who married Helen Edith Stevens at Lower Umgeni, Durban, in July 1914.</li>
      <li>And a Kimberley family older than any of them: <strong>Walter 1899, William 1917, Sarah 1920</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">
      A negative worth keeping: the South African death-registration form of that period names parents
      <strong>only where the deceased was under five</strong>. Erral David Halsey's 1964 form is
      complete, legible, and names neither.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the match list itself now answers to the name <span class="tag doc">Swept the same evening</span></h3>
    <p>
      The search box on Derrick&rsquo;s DNA Matches page takes an <em>ancestral surname</em> and searches
      all 5,464. In August it was given <strong>Leibholz</strong> and <strong>Luwinski</strong> and
      returned <strong>zero</strong> for both. Tonight:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Matches</th><th>What they are</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Castle</strong></td><td><strong>14</strong></td><td>Headed by the 292.7 cM match herself, then thirteen more at <strong>11–38 cM</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Walls</strong></td><td>4</td><td>8–10 cM, and one is a <em>Wallsworth</em>. Nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leibholz</td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td>Unchanged</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Luwinski</td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td>Unchanged</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Stated carefully, because the last time this page got excited about a surname tag it was
      wrong.</strong> The 14 is not proof: the top hit is the Castle descendant already known, and
      thirteen matches at 11–38 cM is the range where a shared surname is suggestive and nothing more.
      What it does do is put <strong>Castle</strong> on the opposite side of the ledger from Leibholz
      and Luwinski, which have never returned a single person.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A trap walked up to and stepped around <span class="tag disp">Checked, not published</span></h3>
    <p>
      Searching the same list for <strong>Wear</strong> returned eight, and the 292.7 cM match was top
      of that list too — which looked, for about a minute, like her tree independently containing the
      Hitchin marriage. <strong>It does not.</strong> Opening her tree and searching it shows the
      &ldquo;Wear&rdquo; in it is <em>Weare</em>: Henry Weare born 1591, Grace Clarke born 1611.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      This is precisely the error this archive published a lesson about in the Figueiredo case —
      believing a surname tag without opening the tree behind it. The lesson held, one day later, on a
      finding it would have been very convenient to believe. <strong>Open the match before believing
      the label.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The lesson, which is the same lesson as last time</h3>
    <p>
      A week went into <strong>Halsey</strong> because it was the surname on the card. It was the wrong
      half of a married couple. The archive had even written down the objection —
      <em>“Halsey is simply the closest match's father's surname … her link to Derrick could as easily
      be her grandmother”</em> — and then carried on down the man's line anyway.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Write the objection down, then act on it.</strong> Two columns on a register settled in
      one glance what a week of surname-chasing could not.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="wear">The segments say two ancestors, not one <span class="tag doc">15 September, evening</span></h2>
  <p>
    Centimorgan totals could not separate the two readings. <strong>The segments could</strong> — and
    they did it in one pass, because they answer a different question: not <em>how much</em> Derrick
    shares with each match, but <em>which parts of his own genome</em> he shares with each.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Between which two of Derrick's matches</th><th>How much of <em>his</em> DNA is common to both</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>333.9 cM &times; 292.7 cM <span class="small muted">(first cousins to each other, 801 cM)</span></td><td><strong>~161 cM</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>292.7 cM &times; 165.9 cM</td><td>~58 cM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>333.9 cM &times; 165.9 cM</td><td>~53 cM</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>292.7 cM &times; 297.5 cM</strong></td><td><strong>~41 cM</strong> — one block, chromosome 2</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>333.9 cM &times; 297.5 cM</strong></td><td><strong>~31 cM</strong> — the same block</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>165.9 cM &times; 297.5 cM</strong></td><td><strong>nothing at all</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Split the closest match's 297.5 cM in two</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>~44 cM</strong> of it lies in regions Derrick also shares with a Castle cousin — a single
      block on chromosome 2, from 173 to 218 Mb, which all four of them carry. That is the Castle
      inheritance, and it behaves exactly as it should.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>~253 cM of it lies nowhere near any of them.</strong> Chromosomes 1, 6, 7, 8, 12, 15, 18,
      20 and 22 — nine segments Derrick shares with her and with <em>no</em> descendant of Agnes Kate
      Castle.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      If Derrick and the closest match had only the Castle couple in common, that 253 cM would have
      nowhere to come from. <strong>They share two ancestors, which is to say they share a married
      couple.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Her great-grandparents on that side are <strong>Henry Robert Wear</strong> and
      <strong>Martha Maud Castle</strong>, married at Hitchin in 1914. Derrick descends from both —
      so <strong>his biological father was their son, and a brother of Cynthia.</strong> Which is,
      word for word, MyHeritage's own most probable relationship for that pair:
      <em>first cousin's daughter</em>, 39.7%.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      And it kills the alternative outright. Under the other reading, Derrick&rsquo;s relationship to
      the closest match would be <em>the same</em> relationship the 292.7 cM match has to her. Hers
      reads <strong>82.1 cM</strong>. His reads <strong>297.5</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Leonard Aubrey Robert Wear <span class="tag inf">Named, 15 September 2026</span></h2>
  <p>
    The 1921 census at Letchworth lists the Wear children: <strong>Leonard Aubrey Robert, born
    1917</strong>, Jeanette Eileen Maud 1919, Kathleen Edith 1921 — and Cynthia follows ten years
    later. A search for Leonard in England returns his birth, that census, and then
    <strong>nothing at all</strong>: no 1939 Register, no marriage, no death, no probate.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Because he was in Johannesburg <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Birth</strong></td><td>WEAR, <strong>Leonard A R</strong> — registered <strong>1917, Hitchin, Hertfordshire</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1921</strong></td><td><strong>72 Pix Road, Letchworth</strong>, aged 4, with Henry Robert and Martha Maud Wear</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>7 Sept 1940</strong></td><td>Married <strong>Phyllis Agnes Robertson</strong>, 25, at <strong>Rosebank, Johannesburg</strong>. He is <strong>23</strong>. Certificate 13 · FamilySearch <code>1:1:6ZCM-N8BV</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1951</strong></td><td><strong>WLD 2026/1951</strong> — divorce, <em>Phyllis Agnes Wear (born Robertson) versus</em> him</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>30 Oct 1951</strong></td><td>Married again, Johannesburg · FamilySearch <code>1:1:6ZHR-R9ZG</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1957</strong></td><td><strong>WLD 790/1957</strong> — <em>opposed application</em>, Leonard Aubrey Robert Wear versus Phyllis Agnes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1962</strong></td><td><strong>WLD 2960/1962</strong> — divorced a second time</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>He was thirty-one in June 1948</strong>, living in Johannesburg, and married to somebody
      else. The marriage ended in a divorce his wife brought in 1951.
    </p>
    <p>
      And he explains a thing this archive could not: <strong>why his sister Cynthia was in
      Johannesburg at all.</strong> She married there in 1952, aged twenty-one, having been born at
      Hitchin. A young woman does not sail from Hertfordshire alone and by chance. Her brother had
      been there for at least twelve years.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What is proven, and what is a strong guess <span class="tag open">Read this part</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Proven to the standard this archive uses for DNA:</strong> Derrick descends from
      <strong>Henry Robert Wear and Martha Maud Castle</strong> — from the couple, not from one of
      them. That is what 253 centimorgans outside every Castle region means, and nothing else explains
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What was not proven, this morning:</strong> that the son in question is Leonard. That
      rested on circumstance — he was the only son the records happened to name, the right age, in the
      right city. <strong>The 1921 census cannot show children born after it.</strong> So the question
      was put properly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>And then the family turned up in the cemetery <span class="tag doc">15 September, late</span></h2>
  <p>
    The Letchworth household was supposed to be the English end of this story. It is not.
    <strong>Johannesburg&rsquo;s burial records hold the parents.</strong>
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Henry Robert Wear</strong>, aged <strong>80</strong></td><td>died <strong>11 September 1969</strong>, buried <strong>West Park Cemetery, Johannesburg</strong>, 13 September</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Martha Maud Wear</strong>, born 1896</td><td>died <strong>16 March 1986</strong>, aged 90, buried Johannesburg</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Bruce Wallace Wear</strong>, born 1936</td><td>married Jillian Anne Thorp at <strong>Kuruman, Cape Province, 21 November 1959</strong> — so the &ldquo;Bruce W&rdquo; of the Hitchin index is <strong>Bruce Wallace</strong>, and he crossed too</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    <strong>The whole household moved.</strong> Leonard was there by 1940, Cynthia married there in
    1952, Bruce married in the Cape in 1959, and the parents died there in 1969 and 1986. The DNA put
    Derrick inside a family in a Hertfordshire market town; that family turns out to have picked itself
    up and moved to the city he was born in.
  </p>

  <h2 id="siblings">Six children, and only two of them sons <span class="tag doc">15 September, late</span></h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>GRO birth index carries the mother's maiden name from September 1911</strong>, which
    means a couple's whole family can be listed directly rather than guessed at from census nights ten
    years apart. Searched: surname <strong>WEAR</strong>, mother's maiden name <strong>CASTLE</strong>,
    all of England and Wales, all years.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Registered</th><th>District</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Leonard A R Wear</strong> — Q1 <strong>1917</strong></td><td>Hitchin</td><td><strong>Son</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Janette E M Wear — Q4 1919</td><td>Hitchin</td><td>Daughter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kathleen E Wear — Q1 1921</td><td>Hitchin</td><td>Daughter</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Cynthia Y E Wear</strong> — Q2 <strong>1931</strong></td><td>Hitchin</td><td>Daughter — married at Johannesburg, 1952</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linda A Wear — Q2 1933</td><td>Hitchin</td><td>Daughter</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Bruce W Wear</strong> — Q3 <strong>1936</strong></td><td>Hitchin</td><td><strong>Son</strong> — <strong>eleven</strong> in June 1948</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Which closes it <span class="tag inf">Inferred, but no longer circumstantially</span></h3>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Six children. Two sons. In June 1948 one of them was thirty-one, and the other was
      eleven.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The DNA says Derrick&rsquo;s biological father was a son of Henry Robert Wear and Martha Maud
      Castle. The index says there were exactly two — <strong>Leonard, born 1917</strong>, and
      <strong>Bruce, born 1936</strong> — and in June 1948 Bruce was eleven. The naming of
      <strong>Leonard Aubrey Robert Wear</strong> no longer rests on him being the only son anybody
      happened to have found.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Two of these six may be living</strong>, and are named anyway, at the direction of the
      family this archive belongs to. They appear here exactly as they appear in the General Register
      Office index and nowhere else &mdash; a name, a quarter, a district &mdash; and nothing further
      about either of them is published. See <a href={base + '/method/'}>the method page</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Limits, because they are real.</strong> The index covers England and Wales only — a child
      born abroad would not appear, though the births run continuously at Hitchin from 1917 to 1936, so
      the family was there throughout. The index does not give sex; if the 1936 child is not a son, the
      conclusion is only stronger. And it clears up a loose end: <strong>Eileen Mary Wear b.1914</strong>
      and <strong>Laurence Anthony Wear b.1915</strong>, both registered at Hitchin, are
      <strong>not</strong> in this list — their mother was not a Castle. A different Wear family in the
      same district.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Two documents would still be better than an inference: <strong>WLD 2026/1951</strong>, the
      divorce, and <strong>WLD 790/1957</strong>, an opposed application brought against his first wife
      six years later — applications of that kind are usually about children. Both are in the National
      Archives at Pretoria and can be ordered by number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the Y cannot help yet <span class="tag disp">Checked, negative, and the negative means little</span></h3>
    <p>
      If this is right, Derrick's Y chromosome is a <strong>Wear</strong> Y, from Henry Robert Wear of
      Bermondsey. So the surname projects were checked. <strong>Of the 7,748 public members of the
      R-U106 project, only 34 sit under R-S11477</strong>, and the surname projects with members there
      are Ballew (16), Stafford (8), Blakely (8), Byrd (5) and York (4). <strong>No Wear, Weir or
      Ware.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      That is not evidence against anything. The branch is barely tested, and its common ancestor lived
      around 200 BCE — ninety generations before any surname existed. <strong>The test that would
      actually settle it is a living male-line Wear descendant taking a Y test</strong>, whose result
      should be Derrick's. That is cheaper and sharper than Big Y-700, though Big Y would at least put
      Derrick on a branch young enough for a surname to mean something.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A correction owed to the person it was sent to <span class="tag disp">Retracted</span></h3>
    <p>
      This morning&rsquo;s message to the 292.7 cM match told her that her tree was wrong and Caroline
      was born a <strong>Walls</strong>, not a Wallis. <strong>Her tree is right.</strong> It has
      <em>Joseph Wallis, 1827&ndash;1912</em>, which matches the 84-year-old widower in the 1911 house
      exactly. The spelling came from a FindMyPast transcription of somebody&rsquo;s handwriting, and
      <strong>a transcription is not a document</strong>. What the census actually adds is that
      Caroline&rsquo;s father was living with them — not a new surname.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="nel">Somebody else had already joined him to a Castle <span class="tag doc">15 September, night</span></h2>
  <p>
    This archive reached <strong>Leonard Aubrey Robert Wear</strong> from the English end: a birth
    registered at Hitchin in 1917, the 1921 census at Letchworth, and a search of the birth index on
    the mother&rsquo;s maiden name. No record anywhere gave him parents — FamilySearch&rsquo;s own tree
    profile for him has none to this day. The link was ours, and it was an inference.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Then the family found this <span class="tag doc">Independent</span></h3>
    <p>
      A small MyHeritage tree — <strong>seven people</strong>, last touched in <strong>May
      2018</strong>, kept by a South African family who know nothing of Baldock, Letchworth or this
      research:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Leonard Wear</strong></td><td>b. <strong>1917, United Kingdom</strong> &middot; d. <strong>1973</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Parents</td><td>&lt;Private&gt; Wear &middot; <strong>&ldquo;Muad&rdquo; Wear, born CASTLE</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wife</td><td>Phyllis Wear, born Robertson, 1915&ndash;2002</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Child</td><td>&lt;Private&gt; Nel, <strong>born Wear</strong> — living</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>&ldquo;Muad&rdquo; is Maud.</strong> Martha <em>Maud</em> Castle, of Baldock,
      Hertfordshire — the woman this archive named from a birth index three days ago, sitting in a
      stranger&rsquo;s tree as Leonard&rsquo;s mother, and put there in 2018.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Two lines of work that never touched each other arrive at the same woman. This is the strongest
      corroboration the Wear reading has had, and it did not come from the DNA at all.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Held at its proper weight.</strong> A user-submitted tree is not a document — it can be
      wrong, and this one disagrees with FamilySearch about when Phyllis died (1973 here, 20 July 2004
      at Key West, Florida there). What it cannot plausibly be is a coincidence: there is no reason for
      an unrelated South African family to give a Leonard Wear born in England in 1917 a mother called
      Maud Castle unless she was.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And it names a living half-sister <span class="tag open">If the reading holds</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Leonard had a daughter.</strong> She is alive, she married a man called Nel, and on the
      reading this archive is testing she is <strong>Derrick&rsquo;s half-sister</strong> — the first
      living person on that side to appear at all.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      She is not named here and nothing about her is published. The person who keeps the tree is
      contactable, and <strong>a DNA test from that household would end the entire question in one
      reading</strong>: half-siblings share around 1,750 cM, and nothing else in genetics looks like it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="dsiblings">Does he have brothers or sisters? <span class="tag open">Asked 15 September</span></h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>On the line of record: none — with one exception that is not on that line at all</h3>
    <p>
      Kurt Israel Luwinski married three times — Frances Hostowsky, Ivone in 1948, and Pauline
      Rappoport in 1956 — and <strong>one child is recorded across all three</strong>. On the paternity
      of record, Derrick has no brother or sister.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exception comes through his <strong>mother</strong>, and so it survives whichever way the
      father question falls: <strong>Henrique Figueiredo Pinto da Rocha, 11 March 1941 &ndash; 27
      October 2022</strong>, Ivone&rsquo;s son by her first marriage. <strong>He is Derrick&rsquo;s
      half-brother on either reading</strong>, because the dispute has only ever been about the father.
      His estate, <strong>12294/2023</strong>, is the file this archive first wrote away for.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>On the DNA line: unknown, and one name that is only a candidate <span class="tag open">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      Leonard was married from <strong>1940 to 1951</strong> and again from <strong>1951 to
      1962</strong>. <strong>WLD 790/1957</strong> — an opposed application he brought against his first
      wife six years after she divorced him — is the kind of case that is usually about children. But
      <strong>no child of his has been documented</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is exactly one name in the Johannesburg records that fits the shape, and it is offered as
      nothing more than that: <strong>Edwina Ann Wear</strong>, born <strong>1945</strong>, died
      <strong>30 June 2014</strong>, cremated at Braamfontein. The right surname, the right city, born
      inside his first marriage. <strong>Her parents are not recorded in anything found so far</strong>,
      and until they are she is a coincidence with good timing.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Everything findable about her has now been searched, and it runs out.</strong> The
      gazette gives her birth date — <strong>28 May 1945</strong> — her identity number
      <strong>4505280044086</strong>, an address at <strong>17 Pollie Street, Homestead,
      Germiston</strong>, and <strong>no surviving spouse</strong>, which on that form means there was
      none. She was <strong>cremated</strong>, not buried: certificate 65676, ashes released on 9 July
      2014 to the undertaker rather than to any relative, so <strong>there is no grave and no stone to
      read</strong>. There is no marriage, no married surname, no obituary, no memorial. Google returns
      nothing for her name at all.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>One document names her parents and it is offline:</strong> the death notice inside estate
      <strong>33157/2014</strong> at the Master of the High Court, Johannesburg. Until it is read the
      honest answer to <em>does Derrick have siblings on this side</em> is: <strong>yes — but she is
      the daughter in the Nel tree, not necessarily this woman.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Working the sons, and one that has to be left alone <span class="tag doc">15 September, evening</span></h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Ernest is out, on a document rather than an argument</h3>
    <p>
      The 1939 Register has him at <strong>29 Norton Crescent, Baldock</strong>:
      <strong>Ernest Castle, born 28 November 1904 — “Butcher (Incapacitated)”</strong>, married, with
      <strong>Mabel</strong> (b. 1908) and children in the house. <em>Incapacitated</em> answers to the
      <em>invalid</em> of the 1911 census, thirty-eight years apart. A married, settled, disabled
      butcher in a Hertfordshire market town in September 1939 is not the man in Johannesburg in June
      1948.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Frederick, and a caution this archive is publishing rather than papering over <span class="tag open">Unproven</span></h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>Frederick Castle married Ella Newman</strong> at Hitchin in the third quarter of
      <strong>1922</strong>, and the 1939 Register puts them at <strong>17 Orchard Road, Baldock</strong>
      — he a <em>vertical driller on aircraft work</em>, she at home, and with them
      <strong>one officially closed record</strong>: a child born after 1912. A son there would be
      exactly what the further reading needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And it may well be the wrong Frederick.</strong> The 1939 Register gives his birth as
      <strong>10 April 1887</strong>. The 1901 household gives Frederick as <strong>eleven</strong> —
      born about 1890 — sitting beside a sister <strong>Ada aged fourteen</strong>. Two siblings born
      three months apart is not possible. And the Hitchin birth index contains
      <strong>no Frederick Castle born between 1889 and 1892 at all</strong>; what it contains is
      <strong>Frederick James Castle, born 1887</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      So either a census age is wrong, or <strong>there are two Castle families in Baldock</strong> and
      this is the other one. The archive does not know which, and is not going to decide it by
      preference. It is written down here so that the next person does not inherit it as a fact.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>An objection worth taking seriously</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>“Kurt was twelve before his parents married. What if Jacob wasn't his father — could Kurt still be Derrick's?” <span class="tag inf">Asked 15 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      It is the best objection anyone has put to this page, because it attacks the right joint. Almost
      everything here assumes Kurt was Jacob's son. He was <strong>born on 11 May 1912 to an unmarried
      Auguste Luwinski</strong>, carried <strong>her</strong> surname, and his parents did not marry
      until <strong>5 January 1924</strong> — twelve years later. Jacob's paternity of Kurt was never
      documented at the birth. So: if Jacob were not Kurt's biological father, Kurt would carry no
      Ashkenazi ancestry, his Y-chromosome would be some other man's, and <strong>both of this page's
      main arguments would collapse.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It fails, and it fails on Auguste rather than on Jacob.</strong> Whoever fathered Kurt,
      <strong>Auguste was still his mother</strong> — that is the one thing the 1912 entry does record,
      and it is why he bore her name. So if Kurt were Derrick's father, Auguste would be Derrick's
      grandmother, and roughly a quarter of Derrick would be her: <strong>East Prussian</strong>, from
      Sauerbaum and Allenstein.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Derrick's estimate, as reported</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Portuguese</td><td><strong>60.6%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>English</td><td><strong>18.5%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>French · Danish · Breton</td><td>5.8% · 5.4% · 5.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dutch · Scottish and Welsh</td><td>1.5% · 1.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Germanic</strong></td><td><strong>1.4%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ashkenazi Jewish</strong></td><td><strong>0.0%</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>There is no room for a German grandmother.</strong> Every non-Portuguese percentage points
      at the Atlantic and the North Sea — English, French, Danish, Breton, Dutch — some 38% of him,
      against <strong>1.4% Germanic</strong>. MyHeritage even names his additional genetic groups as
      <em>“Southeastern and Eastern England”</em> and <em>“England”</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>So the objection does not rescue Kurt.</strong> It would have had to survive two
      independent tests and it only addresses one: rewriting Kurt's father leaves Kurt's mother exactly
      where she was, and Auguste is not in Derrick's DNA either.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>But the question underneath it is a real one, and it is already in the post <span class="tag open">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Was Jacob Leibholz actually Kurt's biological father?</strong> That is a separate question
      from Derrick's paternity, and this archive cannot answer it from DNA — there is no sample from
      Kurt and none from any Leibholz. But the paper is thinner than the family tree suggests, and one
      detail is genuinely odd.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under German law a child born to an unmarried mother took <em>her</em> surname, and
      <em>Legitimation durch nachfolgende Ehe</em> — legitimation by the parents' subsequent marriage —
      would ordinarily have made him a <strong>Leibholz</strong> in 1924. <strong>He stayed a Luwinski,
      to his death in 1968</strong>, and registered his own son under that name. Meanwhile his brother
      <strong>Siegfried, born in August 1925 after the marriage, was a Leibholz</strong> from the start.
    </p>
    <p>
      That asymmetry has an innocent explanation — a family that simply never bothered to change a
      grown boy's name — and a less innocent one. <strong>The archive does not know which</strong>, and
      the 1912 birth entry is the document that decides it: a marginal note of
      <em>Vaterschaftsanerkennung</em> or legitimation, or the absence of one.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>It was asked for on 15 September</strong>, as the third question in the letter to the
      <a href={base + '/status/'}>Landesarchiv Berlin</a> — before this objection was raised, and for
      the same reason. If the margin is blank, then Kurt's descent from Jacob rests on nothing but
      family memory, and this archive will say so.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The replies were already in the inbox <span class="tag disp">Found by accident, 15 September 2026</span></h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Every one of them had been written to in June 2025, and three answered</h3>
    <p>
      Opening MyHeritage's message window to send a carefully drafted first approach revealed that the
      matches had <strong>all been contacted fifteen months earlier</strong>, with one template — and
      that three of them had replied with names. <strong>Nothing in this archive had used any of it.</strong>
      It was found by accident, and it is the worst process failure of the project.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Two things follow at once. The non-paternity was <strong>already disclosed to these people in
      2025</strong>, so the delicacy being written into fresh drafts was, to that extent, beside the
      point. And the original template <strong>asked the wrong question</strong> — it asked each of them
      to work out how they were related and to share a tree, which is labour, rather than telling them
      something they could react to.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What the three said</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>The second cousin at 333.9 cM, 18 June 2025 — and he offered everything.</strong>
      <em>“You are very welcome to all the information I have — do you have access to Ancestry? Or I
      could draw it out. It seems likely Derek and I are related through Great Grandparents … My
      Paternal Great Grandparents are Harrison / Randall and Crisp and Bray.”</em> He was never
      answered. It also explains why his MyHeritage tree holds three people: <strong>his real tree is
      on Ancestry.</strong> And because his <em>paternal</em> great-grandparents are Harrison, Randall,
      Crisp and Bray, the <strong>Brown</strong> in his surname list must be maternal — which is
      exactly what the Brown–Castle reading requires.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And one remark that does not fit.</strong> He checked Salkow, de Witt, Luwinski and Rocha
      against his own matches, and reported that <em>“Rocha was the only name which appeared.”</em>
      Rocha is <a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Ivone's</a> side — the maternal, Portuguese one. Either he has
      an unrelated Portuguese connection, or the tidy split of this archive's two clusters is wrong
      somewhere. <strong>It is the one loose thread in the whole DNA picture, and it should be put to
      him directly.</strong> <span class="tag open">Open</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The closest match of all, 17 June 2025 — and she named four grandparents.</strong> Her
      maternal grandparents were <strong>John and Elsie Parr</strong>; her paternal grandparents
      <strong>Mervin and Cynthia Halsey</strong>, Cynthia born <strong>Weir</strong>, her mother
      possibly <strong>Mord</strong>. Since her common ancestors with Derrick are <em>his own
      grandparents</em>, one of those couples ought to be the join — most plausibly the Halseys.
      <strong>Four new surnames to work: Halsey, Parr, Weir, Mord.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And the sentence worth staring at</strong>, from the match at 165.9 cM, also 17 June 2025:
      his great-grandfather was <em>“<strong>Walley Brown, who was married twice (possibly at the same
      time)</strong>”</em> — and this was <strong>on his mother's side</strong>. A Brown
      great-grandfather, maternal, married twice and perhaps bigamously, is precisely the shape of thing
      that leaves a branch nobody has traced. And <em>Walley</em> may well be <strong>Walter</strong> —
      the big tree's grandfather is <strong>Walter George Brown, 1889–1971</strong>. He added that
      <strong>his sister keeps the family tree</strong> and he would pass the message on. Nobody
      followed up with her either.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What it changes <span class="tag doc">Method</span></h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No cold letters.</strong> Everyone in the British cluster already has an open thread. The follow-up sent today went into the existing one, because the drafted version opened <em>“I hope you don't mind a message out of the blue”</em> — which would have been false.</li>
      <li><strong>Answer the man who offered his tree, first.</strong> Fifteen months late is better than never, and the offer may still stand.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask for the sister</strong> who keeps the other family's tree.</li>
      <li><strong>Put the Rocha question directly.</strong> It is the only fact on the table that the two-cluster model cannot absorb.</li>
      <li>The drafted letter to the closest match is <strong>obsolete</strong> — she has already answered the question it asks.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Read the inbox before writing to it.</strong> A day went into composing careful first
      approaches to people who had already been approached, three of whom had already replied with names.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What would settle it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><s><strong>Y-DNA.</strong> The paternal line, tested directly.</s> <strong>Done, 14 September 2026: R-S11477.</strong> See below.</li>
    <li><s><strong>The British cluster.</strong></s> <strong>Done — see above.</strong> It gave the surnames and the city. What remains is a message to the closest match, which this archive will not send on anyone's behalf.</li>
    <li><s><strong>Shared-match triangulation.</strong></s> <strong>Done — see above.</strong> One family, and the nearest surname on it is Halsey.</li>
    <li><strong>A search of all 5,464</strong> for Leibholz, Luwinski, Thimm and Fricke. Absence across the whole list, and not merely the top of it, would be conclusive.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>The Y-DNA came back: R-S11477</h2>
  <p>
    Consent was given on Derrick's own kit on <strong>14 September 2026</strong> and the result
    appeared the same day. This is the test the archive had nominated as the one that would break the
    finding or confirm it. <span class="tag doc">Documented</span>
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>R-S11477 — R1b, and specifically the North Sea branch</h3>
    <p>
      MyHeritage's own page puts it plainly: <em>“R-S11477 is a human paternal lineage nested within
      the broader <strong>R-Z381</strong> haplogroup. Every man who carries it descends from a single
      ancestor who lived around 200 BCE.”</em> The haplogroup-family badge on the same page reads
      <strong>R1b / Z381</strong>, and the node above it on the time tree is <strong>R-FGC13328</strong>,
      about 600 BCE.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chain is <strong>R1b → M269 → P311 → U106 → Z381 → FGC13328 → S11477</strong>, and the branch
      that matters is <strong>U106</strong>. U106 is the <strong>North Sea, Germanic</strong> branch of
      R1b: it peaks in the <strong>Netherlands at about 37%</strong>, and runs at roughly
      <strong>21% in Germany and Austria</strong> and <strong>18% in England and Denmark</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ashkenazi paternal lines do not look like this.</strong> Haplogroup <strong>J</strong>
      alone accounts for about <strong>38%</strong> of the Ashkenazi paternal gene pool; the
      characteristic lineages are J1, J2, E1b1b, R1a and G. R1b does occur among Ashkenazi men — but
      the literature describes R1b-M269 there as <em>repeated punctuated introgression of European
      Y chromosomes</em>, and the two Ashkenazi R1b clusters that are identifiably Near Eastern belong
      to <strong>R1b-Z2103</strong>. A different branch of R1b entirely. Not U106.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>Where the branch lives</h3>
  <p class="small">
    Self-reported earliest known paternal origin, from the 74 R-S11477 testers MyHeritage can see.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>R-S11477 testers</th><th>Total testers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>United States</td><td><strong>29</strong></td><td>30,253</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>England</strong></td><td><strong>19</strong></td><td>27,563</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Germany</td><td>7</td><td>17,457</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scotland</td><td>3</td><td>14,026</td></tr>
      <tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>3</td><td>9,712</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Northern Ireland</td><td>2</td><td>2,869</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Russian Federation</td><td>2</td><td>7,172</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Netherlands</td><td>2</td><td>2,289</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Finland · France · Norway · Sweden · Ireland · Poland · Estonia</td><td>1 each</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    <strong>British Isles 28. United States 29. Germany 7.</strong> No Israel, and none of the
    countries that dominate Ashkenazi paternal testing. And note where the two independent systems
    meet: the autosomal cluster is <strong>British with a Johannesburg branch</strong>, and the
    Y-haplogroup's largest European population is <strong>England</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What it proves — and what it does not <span class="tag inf">Read this before quoting it</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>This is not a comparison with Jacob Leibholz's Y-chromosome.</strong> The archive does
      not have Jacob's, and it almost certainly never will, because his male line is exhausted:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Jacob's male line</th><th>Where it ends</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Kurt → <strong>Derrick</strong></td><td>Kurt's only son of record is the man whose paternity is the question</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Siegfried → <strong>Michael Kurt Leibholz</strong></td><td>b. 15 November 1951, <strong>d. 3 November 2005</strong>, no children recorded</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Samuel</strong></td><td>Murdered at Auschwitz, 1943. No sons recorded</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Oskar</strong></td><td>Died at Theresienstadt, 1944. No sons recorded</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Georg → Werner, b. 1913</td><td>And this archive already argues <a href={base + '/leibholz/'}>Georg was not Jacob's brother</a> — so Werner's Y would not be Jacob's either</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      So the Y-DNA works by <strong>character, not by comparison</strong>. It says that Derrick's
      unbroken father-to-father line belongs to the branch of R1b that peaks in the Netherlands and
      England, and not to any lineage characteristic of Ashkenazi Jewish paternal descent.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>On its own that would not be decisive.</strong> A Jewish family can carry a European
      Y-chromosome — that is exactly what “European introgression” means, and it is why nobody should
      argue from a haplogroup alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>But it is not on its own.</strong> It is the second independent system to return the same
      answer. The autosomal estimate says 0.0% Ashkenazi where a quarter is required. The Y-chromosome
      says the paternal line is North Sea R1b. Different molecules, different inheritance, no
      contradiction between them, and no evidence anywhere pointing the other way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The sweep of all 5,464, finally done</h2>
  <p>
    This was the archive's oldest outstanding item, and every earlier attempt was beaten by the
    filter interface. The tool was there all along: with the right kit selected, the DNA Matches page
    has a search box labelled <em>“Name or ancestral surname”</em>, which searches each match's own
    name <strong>and</strong> the ancestral surnames declared in their tree, across the whole list.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Leibholz: nothing. Luwinski: nothing. <span class="tag doc">Exhaustive</span></h3>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Searched across all 5,464 matches</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Leibholz</strong></td><td><strong>No DNA Matches found</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Luwinski</strong></td><td><strong>No DNA Matches found</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      Not one of the 5,464 people who share measurable DNA with Derrick carries either name or lists
      it among their ancestors. This page has said from the beginning that there is no German or
      Jewish cluster near the top of the list. That was a statement about six matches opened in full.
      <strong>It is now a completed search.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The Portuguese half of that sweep was overstated, and is withdrawn <span class="tag disp">Self-corrected</span></h3>
    <p>
      For about an hour this page said the same search had found Ivone's family — 27 matches for
      <em>Figueiredo</em>, 13 for <em>Mota</em> — and called it the best lead on her parentage the
      archive had ever had. <strong>That was written before a single one of those matches was opened.</strong>
      Opening them undoes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>“Shared ancestral surname” is a string match.</strong> On the top Figueiredo match — 29.0 cM,
      fifth or fourth cousin — the two people MyHeritage is pairing are <strong>Ivone Mota Figueiredo</strong>,
      born 1924 at Lourenço Marques, and <strong>Águeda Maria de Figueiredo</strong>, the match's direct
      ancestor six generations back, <strong>born before 12 April 1713</strong>. Two centuries and an ocean
      apart, joined on a string, in a country where Figueiredo is one of the commonest names there is.
      The shared <em>place</em> is no better: it resolves to “Portugal”, and beside the match's own parish
      the panel reads <em>“No ancestors in derrick's tree.”</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And the Mota matches share no surname with him at all.</strong> MyHeritage says so on the
      50.1 cM match in plain words. The reason is in Derrick's own indexed surname list —
      <em>Figueiredo, Leibholz, Luwinski, Schmul, Thimm</em> — which <strong>does not contain Mota</strong>,
      because the source tree carries it as a middle name. The thirteen hits were other families' Motas.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      The archive already held much better Portuguese matches, worked weeks ago and still on
      <a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Ivone's page</a>: <strong>108.7 cM</strong> at second cousin's son, and
      <strong>133.2 cM</strong>. A 29 cM surname coincidence was never going to beat them.
      <strong>The lesson is cheap and worth keeping: open the match before believing the label.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What the sweep did establish <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>The two zeroes stand, and they are stronger than they looked.</strong> MyHeritage indexes
      <strong>Leibholz</strong> and <strong>Luwinski</strong> as two of Derrick's own five ancestral
      surnames. So the search was not firing blanks at terms the system had never heard of — it had real,
      indexed names to match, and across 5,464 people it returned nothing for either.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And there is no computed path to anybody.</strong> Filtering his matches for
      <em>Has Theory of Family Relativity</em> returns <em>“There are no DNA Matches that fit your
      criteria.”</em> MyHeritage has built no relationship path to a single one of the 5,464. Whatever is
      found here will be found by hand. <span class="tag doc">Negative, and clean</span>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      One loose thread, recorded as loose. The two Portuguese matches that were opened both trace to the
      <strong>Viseu district</strong> — Treixedo in Santa Comba Dão, and Tondela and Castro Daire, some
      45 km apart along the Dão. Worth noting; not worth believing. Both are distant, one has no
      documented link to Ivone at all, and <strong>the closest Portuguese match by a wide margin records
      no place more specific than “Portugal”.</strong> If Viseu were the family's district, the strongest
      match ought to show it. <span class="tag open">Open</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Can the Y line be named from the men who share it?</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Yes — but not from this result, and the reason is arithmetic <span class="tag inf">Tested</span></h3>
    <p>
      FamilyTreeDNA's Discover pages are public, and they list the projects whose members carry
      R-S11477: <strong>Ballew</strong> (16 members), <strong>Stafford</strong> (8),
      <strong>Blakely</strong> (8), <strong>Byrd</strong> (5), <strong>York</strong> (4), plus a Norman
      England group and a German-language group. Real surnames, English and Anglo-American, on
      Derrick's own paternal branch.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>They cannot name his father, because surnames are about 800 years old and this branch is
      about 2,200.</strong> R-S11477's common ancestor lived around 200 BCE. Every Ballew and Stafford
      on it is a cousin at roughly ninety generations, descended from a man who lived before any of
      those names existed.
    </p>
    <p>That is the theory. It was then tested against Derrick's own match list:</p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Y-branch surname</th><th>Matches in Derrick's 5,464</th><th>Closest</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ballew</td><td>2</td><td>5th cousin, 0.3% (23.4 cM)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stafford</td><td>13</td><td>5th cousin, 0.3% (22.1 cM)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blakely</td><td>6</td><td>5th cousin, 0.4% (28.4 cM)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Byrd</td><td>15</td><td>5th cousin, 0.3% (21.5 cM)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Yorke</td><td>2</td><td>4th cousin's son, 0.2% (17.0 cM)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>Every one lands between 10 and 28 cM, at the noise floor</strong>, and not one is near the
      close British cluster at 297–334 cM. The prediction was that these surnames would be
      genealogically meaningless. The match list agrees.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>What would work:</strong> a <strong>Big Y-700</strong>, which would place Derrick on one
      of the <strong>42 branches below R-S11477</strong> — terminal branches in a well-tested lineage
      are often only three to six centuries old, and at that depth a shared branch usually does mean a
      shared surname. It would also open <strong>FamilyTreeDNA's Y-DNA match list</strong>, which is the
      literal answer to the question and which <strong>MyHeritage does not provide at all</strong>: a
      list of men sharing the paternal line, each with their earliest known ancestor's name, year and
      birthplace.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>But the Y is not the best tool available here, and it is worth saying so.</strong> The
      Halsey match is Derrick's <strong>first cousin's daughter</strong> at <strong>297.5 cM</strong> —
      which means their common ancestors are <strong>Derrick's biological grandparents</strong>. The
      surname being looked for is that grandfather's, and it is in her family tree, not in anybody's
      chromosome. The Harrison match is a <strong>second cousin</strong> at <strong>333.9 cM</strong>,
      in the United Kingdom. <strong>Two messages could produce a name; a Big Y produces a branch
      number and a probability.</strong> Both are worth doing, in that order.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The label does not change, and that is the point <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      The <a href={base + '/method/'}>method page</a> has said since this archive was built that the
      paternity finding <em>stays Inferred until a Y-DNA result moves it</em>. The result has been run.
      It did not overturn the finding — it corroborated it from an independent direction — and there is
      no further test available, because Jacob's male line has ended.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>So the tag stays <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span>.</strong> There is still no
      document and still no direct comparison, and a finding does not become Documented because it
      survived a test. What has changed is that the test nominated to break it was run, and it held.
      The archive can now say so without hedging.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Two things would sharpen it further. <strong>Big Y-700</strong> — MyHeritage calls its own result
      a low-resolution estimate; a full sequence would pinpoint the terminal branch and place Derrick in
      a surname project, and the nearest surname in the British cluster is <strong>Halsey</strong>. And
      <strong>one message</strong>: if any of the Halsey, Harrison or Browne matches is a man descending
      father-to-father from that family, his Y is directly comparable to Derrick's, and that costs
      nothing at all.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
